WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:10.030
There is one simple fact that has yet to penetrate
very deeply into the policy world.

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:14.360
Our economic future, like that of other nations
of the world, depends directly on the quality

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:15.590
of our workers.

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:21.060
More specifically, in this increasingly technological
age, the cognitive skills of future workers

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.960
will determine much of our economic wellbeing.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:29.029
Given that simple fact, the backlash against
testing in schools and against accountability

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:31.480
for results is a bit scary.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:36.440
From international testing in math, we find
that U.S. students rank 39th in the world,

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:39.620
putting us below the average for developed countries

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:42.560
and having us competing with Croatia and Greece.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:46.480
You might ask, isn’t our economy still the
envy of the world?

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:50.530
And the answer is “yes for now”, but not necessarily
in the future.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:55.640
We have been able to benefit from good economic
institutions with limited governmental intrusion,

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:59.400
with more schooling than elsewhere, and with
high-skilled immigrants.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:03.820
But the future strength of each is in doubt,
and we will have to rely on the skills of

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:06.440
our population for ongoing success.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:11.780
Do you think that it is possible for our schools to compete with the best in the world?

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:13.780
Let us know in your comments.

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:18.720
Also, let us know what other topics you'd like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds,

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.900
and be sure to like and subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

